## Context

Child 1 of tracker 1226 (optimistic merge, option C). Purely additive: today's
full gate record keeps running before the merge until child 1245 lands, so
main is never untested in between. Design in 1226 § Design; this ticket
carries its after-merge half.

## Design

- **Runner** (new script, launched in the background by the merging session
  after the read-back): takes a host-local `flock` (the kernel frees it when
  the holder dies; no pid file). Lock busy: exit at once, the holder will see
  the new tip. Holding it: run the after set on the current `origin/main`
  tip, write the record as a note on that tip, fetch; if main moved, loop on
  the newest tip until quiet. A result covers its tip and every tip it passed
  over.
- **Scope line** of an after-merge record differs from a before-merge one, so
  neither `check` nor the guard hook can take one for the other.
- **Red:** open a ticket naming the tip, the failing checks and the
  first-parent merges since the last green after-record. Main is read-only,
  so the ticket lands through a ticket-only PR merged on the bookkeeping
  path. That merge starts a runner on a tip still red: **dedupe** — no new
  ticket while an open one already names the same failing checks. Nothing
  reverts automatically; no flaky re-run (a red check is red).
- **Audit:** `scripts/main_sweep.py` reports main tips with no
  after-merge record and runs the after set on the latest one.
- Other hosts: a merge from another machine starts its own runner; tolerated
  (one wasted run, never a wrong record).

Out: everything 1226 § Design lists as out; changing what runs before the
merge (child 1245).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] After a merge, the runner runs the full set on the main tip in the background and writes its record as a note on that tip, under an after-merge scope line.
- [ ] One runner at a time via `flock`: a test with three quick merges shows at most two runs, the last on the newest tip; a killed runner leaves no lock behind.
- [ ] A red after-merge record opens a ticket naming the tip, the failing checks and the merges since the last green tip; a test shows it on a planted failure, and a second red run on the same failure opens no second ticket.
- [ ] The daily audit reports main tips with no after-merge record and runs the set on the latest one; a test shows it.
- [ ] `skills/merge/SKILL.md` step 8 (or after) starts the runner; the harness gate passes.

## Raid annotation (Imagine+Plan, 2026-10-27)

- **Why now / premise.** Holds against the tree: `.merge-gate.json` still has `tiers`, `scripts/merge-gate.py` has no after-merge path, `main_sweep.py` audits only before-merge receipts (candidate notes), `skills/merge/SKILL.md` ends at step 8 Sync. Gate records are `refs/notes/gate` notes keyed on an object id; `decide()` matches `candidate=<oid>` in `revision` and requires `scope` in `accepted_scopes`. 1245 and tracker 1226 both wait on this ticket. Verdict GO, no PREMISE-OBJECTION.
- **Simplest path.** `flock(1)` or `fcntl.flock` on one file under `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/idh/` (the main-guard journal already lives there), no daemon, no pid file. Reuse `merge-gate.py` (`format_entry`, `append_and_push`, `run_checks`) by adding one subcommand `run-after`; no new record format. Scope line = `<scope>/after-merge`, so `decide()` with the before-merge scopes fails it (`check` and the hook cannot mistake it). Antipattern scan: no lock library, no queue, no retry, no revert, no flaky re-run; do not generalize into a "post-merge hooks" framework (premature).
- **Actions, in order.**
  1. Test first (below), then `run-after` in `scripts/merge-gate.py`: flock NB, fetch, loop `run_checks` on a throwaway worktree of the current `origin/main` tip, note on that tip, refetch, repeat until the tip is stable.
  2. Red path: a function building the ticket text from `git log --first-parent` since the last green after-record; dedupe key = sorted failing check names, stored as a branch name `after-red-<hash>` (checked with `git ls-remote`, covers an in-flight PR) plus a marker line in the ticket body (checked against `tickets/` on `origin/main`).
  3. `scripts/main_sweep.py`: list first-parent tips since the last after-record, run `run-after` on the latest one; new exit/summary line.
  4. `skills/merge/SKILL.md` step 8 (or a new step 9): start `run-after` in the background after sync.
  5. Files: `scripts/merge-gate.py`, `scripts/main_sweep.py`, `skills/merge/SKILL.md`, `tests/test_after_merge.py` (new), `docs/2026-10-21-main-update-guard.md` (one line: after-record never admits a main update).
- **First failing test.** `test_three_quick_merges_run_at_most_twice`: temp bare origin + clone, a config whose single check appends the tip to a log file and sleeps; a mover thread pushes two more commits during run 1; assert the log has at most 2 lines and the last is the final origin tip. Dependencies: none (1245 stays blocked on this).
- **Red ticket landing without a human.** Create the file `tickets/NNNN-after-merge-red-<tip>.erg` on a branch, push, `gh pr create`, then merge by the bookkeeping tier of `/merge` (a ticket-only diff matches `BOOKKEEPING`, needs a before-merge record, no `/gaze`). Safe because the diff is one new file under the bookkeeping allowlist, enforced by `merge-gate.py tier` (a runner that sees any other path in its diff stops and prints). Residual risk: the runner holds no session, so it needs the forge credentials that ticket 1196 says sessions lose (open and unresolved); on missing credentials it must fail loud (print the ticket text and exit 1), never silent. The merge of that PR starts a runner on a still-red tip: covered by the dedupe key.
- **Tests avoid a real forge.** The forge calls (`gh pr create`, `gh pr merge`) sit behind one injectable command (`IDH_FORGE_CMD`, default `gh`); tests point it at a script that records its argv and does a local `git merge` into the bare origin. Everything else is real git in `tmp_path`.
- **Monster check.** One sign-off unit, about 5 files and 300 lines, five criteria that share one record format. If the red-ticket autoland proves heavy in review, split it as child "red-ticket PR" (criterion 3 only) after the runner and audit land; the audit and the record do not depend on it. Not required now.
- **Tautological-test check.** Do not mock `flock`: use two real processes. "Killed runner leaves no lock": SIGKILL a subprocess holding the lock, then acquire it; this fails if a lock file with a pid were used. Red test must plant a failing check in the config and assert on the generated ticket text derived from real `git log`, not on a stubbed ticket. Dedupe test: run twice, count branches on the fake origin and ticket files; the stub forge must not hold the dedupe logic. Audit test: build tips with and without notes and assert the listed shas, not a count alone.
- **Shared files.** `skills/merge/SKILL.md` and `scripts/merge-gate.py` collide with 1245 (blocked, serial) and 1243 (merge commit attribution). `scripts/main_sweep.py` and `docs/2026-10-21-main-update-guard.md` are touched by 1245 only. 1237, 1227, 1239, 1246 and 1240 do not touch the merge-gate files as far as their titles say (adherence parsing, gaze scripts, lair report, raid sitrep, doctor visit); possible overlap on `skills/merge/SKILL.md` with 1227 if the closing script is called from it, and on STATE.md or `tests/` helpers: rebase before the gate.

## Raid feasibility (2026-10-27)
WARN (criterion 3 infeasible as designed; shared files). `format_entry`, `append_and_push`, `run_checks`, `decide`, BOOKKEEPING and `main_sweep.py` exist as claimed, and so do the docs file and merge recipe step 8.
- Blind spot: the bookkeeping tier in `.merge-gate.json` runs `validate-tickets`, `agnostic-guard` and `pytest-guard` on the candidate (main tip plus the ticket file). When the after-merge red is `pytest-guard` (the likely case), the red-ticket PR fails its own before-merge record and can never merge on the bookkeeping path. The autoland deadlocks exactly when it fires. Check: plant a failing test on a temp main and run `merge-gate.py run` on a ticket-only candidate. Remedy: split criterion 3 now, as the plan's own fallback allows, and have the runner print or file the red outside a merge (for example a forge issue behind an extension point). Its ticket then lands with the fix.
- Shared files: the locked worktree `explore-merge-skill` holds an unlanded author trim (9ecd4c52 plus uncommitted edits) of `skills/merge/SKILL.md` (-56 lines), the `scripts/merge-gate.py` docstring, `rules/git.md` (Gating tiers moved there), `skills/raid/SKILL.md` and 4 tests. Ask the author whether to land or drop it before 1244 edits merge SKILL step 8. Otherwise 1244 will conflict with it.
